course: dictionary
lesson: 15865
title: Un- words and glaze
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The prefix un- means not, so unglazed means not glazed. A glaze is a thin, shiny, glassy coating put on pottery. «The potter left the little bowl unglazed, so it was dull and rough.» The clues are dull and rough: without its glaze, the bowl does not shine.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The old cottage had unglazed windows, so the wind blew straight in.» There is no glass in those windows, so the wind gets through. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Unglazed fits, and it means without its glassy covering or glass.
explain: «The gardener filled the unglazed terracotta pot with soil.» Terracotta is a clay pot, and an unglazed one has no shiny coating. Try your meaning in the sentence: "filled the not-glazed pot with soil" still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Un- is a prefix that means not, so unglazed means not glazed.
fact: A glaze is a thin, shiny, glassy coating on pottery.
fact: «Unglazed» means having no shiny glassy coating, so it looks dull and feels rough.
fact: «Unglazed» can also describe a window or door with no glass in it.
fact: Clay pots are often fired unglazed first, then glazed and fired again to make them shiny and waterproof.
exercise: «The bowl was unglazed, so it looked dull and felt rough.» What does unglazed mean? | *With no shiny glassy coating | Covered in bright paint | Very heavy and cold | Full of water
exercise: The prefix un- in unglazed means what? | Under | *Not | Very | Again
exercise: «The old house had unglazed windows and wooden shutters.» What is missing from the windows? | *Glass | Curtains | Paint | Shutters
test: «The unglazed terracotta pot let air and water through.» Why? | It was painted gold | Someone put a lid on it | *Its clay is open, with no glassy coating to stop it | It was made of glass
test: What is a glaze? | *A shiny, glassy coating on pottery | A kind of clay | A tool for cutting | A type of plant
exam: Which sentence uses unglazed correctly? | «The window was unglazed, so the shiny glass sparkled.» | *«The window was unglazed, so the rain blew straight in.» | «The unglazed bowl was very heavy and full of water.» | «The unglazed bowl sparkled in the sun like glass.»
